      "content": "The \"goblin trap\" was just a figure of speech, a way to illustrate the complexity of the deadlock situation. In reality, the issue is likely due to a resource contention problem, where multiple transactions are competing for access to the same database resources.\n\nThe deadlock is caused by the concurrent requests to the inventory_update_service call, which are trying to acquire locks on the same database resources. This is a classic case of a \"pessimistic locking\" issue, where the system is holding onto locks for too long, preventing other transactions from accessing the resources.\n\nTo fix the deadlock, you can try the following steps:\n\n1. Review the database schema and identify any bottlenecks or contention points. Are there any tables or resources that are being accessed concurrently by multiple transactions?\n2. Consider implementing a more fine-grained locking strategy, such as using row-level locks or optimistic concurrency control.\n3. Optimize the database queries to reduce the number of locks required. For example, you can try to batch multiple updates together or use a more efficient locking mechanism.\n4. Increase the timeout values for the inventory_update_service call to give the system more time to resolve the deadlock.\n5. Consider implementing a retry mechanism for the inventory_update_service call, to",
